  <h2>Memory</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>hbm_model</strong> is a 2,048-word memory: Q, K and V rows, the output, and the NAIVE score and probability tiles. It counts the bytes read and written.</li>
    <li><strong>dma</strong> moves one burst at a time, with a configurable first-word latency (0 to 64 cycles) and one word per cycle after it.</li>
    <li><strong>K/V buffers</strong> are two K tiles and two V tiles, each 4 rows. Without overlap only the first pair is used. With overlap, the second pair is filled while the first is in use.</li>
    <li><strong>sram_bank</strong> stages output rows before the DMA writes them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Controllers</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>flash_ctrl</strong> loops over Q blocks and, inside each block, over the K/V tiles. It issues the DMA loads, the
    weight loads and the group tiles, skips tiles above the causal diagonal, and normalizes and stores each block.
    <strong>naive_ctrl</strong> runs the four passes of the standard softmax over each row block, writing and reading the
    score and probability tiles through HBM.
  </p>

  <h2>Tile core</h2>
  <p>
    <code>attn_tile</code> processes one 4-row group against one K/V tile. The two 4×4 systolic arrays are the matrix
    products. Between them sit the scale-and-mask unit, the row maximum, the exp2 unit and the sum. The O accumulator
    rescales the running output by α and adds the new product. Five operations share the datapath: FLASH (online),
    and the four NAIVE passes.
  </p>

  <h2>Timing</h2>
  <p>
    The schedule is exact: busy cycles follow the model in <code>sim/golden/schedule.py</code>, and the end-to-end test
    checks them for every configuration it runs. Overlap changes the schedule, so its cycle counts are pinned as
    regression baselines in <code>sim/perf_baseline.json</code>.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The design has not been synthesized for an FPGA yet (Phase 10). The numbers on this site come from simulation.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
